From 1d7d65a38188b493ec7bd1ea43ef2d7cc4022a94 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 23:51:22 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Save states: the running game's whole state, saved and put
 back

host/snapshot.hpp saves and restores a run between two dispatches, for
rollback netplay: guest RAM and VRAM (copied back in place, since generated
code points into them), the running thread's context, the runtime's
starvation phase and thread identity, the kernel's threads, objects, waits,
clock, vblank schedule, memory blocks, guest calls and interrupts
(Kernel::save_state), and what participants add (snapshot::add). The HLE
modules add their own: the display, controller latch, GE lists and state,
audio channels (not the device's cursor) and SAS voices; threadman's pools,
pipes and lightweight mutexes; open files' positions and pending results;
and ATRAC contexts. An ATRAC decoder is not copied: it stays where it is,
and after a restore the frames it made since the save are handed out again
in the order it made them, since a seek's warm-up is close to straight
decoding but not the same to the bit. Restoring restarts the hold to real
time.

Tested with Dissidia 012 on Windows (clang): a recorded versus battle
replayed headless with a restore 8 pad reads back at every 9th read (17,800
reads run twice) and at every 60th, and with player 2's input wrong on each
first run, matched a plain replay in all of guest RAM at every read. A save
took about 5 to 6 ms and a restore about 2.3 ms on this PC. Not tested:
other games, Linux, with drawing on, a state saved during a load or a
dialog, a host file closed after a save (its handle does not come back).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
